      "title": "Landlocked Ethiopia, Unstable Red Sea, and Geopolitical battle in the Horn of Africa - Borkena",
      "link": "https://borkena.com/landlocked-ethiopia-unstable-red-sea-and-geopolitical-battle-in-the-horn-of-africa/",
      "date": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 13:52:27 GMT",
      "image": "https://borkena.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Ethiopia-_-Horn-of-Africa-_-Red-Sea.jpg",
      "content": "Landlocked Ethiopia, Unstable Red Sea, and Geopolitical battle in the Horn of Africa Home Featured Latest News Business Opinion Video Amharic Entertainment Restaurant Travel Sport Books Blog Business Listings Jobs Support Us Contact Search borkena Ethiopian News and Informed Opinion. Truth Matters! Sign in Welcome! Log into your account your username your password Forgot your password? Get help Create an account Create an account Welcome! Register for an account your email your username A password will be e-mailed to you. Password recovery Recover your password your email A password will be e-mailed to you. borkena Ethiopian News and Opinion Home Featured Latest News Business Opinion Video Amharic Entertainment Restaurant Travel Sport Books Blog Business Listings Jobs Support Us Contact Sign in / Join Monday, October 26, 2026 Facebook Linkedin Telegram TikTok WhatsApp Instagram X Youtube Home Opinion Landlocked Ethiopia, Unstable Red Sea, and Geopolitical battle in the Horn of... Opinion October 26, 2026 Updated: October 26, 2026 Landlocked Ethiopia, Unstable Red Sea, and Geopolitical battle in the Horn of Africa October 26, 2026 Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp Linkedin Telegram Viber Landlocked Ethiopia, Unstable Red Sea, and Strategic battle in the Horn By Metta-Alem Sinishaw Washington, DC Executive Summary Ethiopia currently faces multi-front insurgencies, severe economic distress, and a legitimacy deficit which collectively poses a serious threat to national survival. The insurgencies are manifestations of a much deeper strategic crisis that needs political reconfiguration. Ethiopia’s internal political crisis, its quest for access to the Sea, and the dispute over Nile water resources are increasingly intertwined. Ethiopia’s quest for access to the Sea is a legitimate and pragmatic effort aimed at building regional integration. Dependence of a country with over 130 million people, expanding economy, and asymmetrical military prowess on a single port increases vulnerability and undermines Ethiopia’s industrial aspiration. Although a commercially leased port is required, it represents only a partial solution to meeting the expanding operational needs. As the security of the Red Sea affects Ethiopia much more acutely than any of its neighbors combined, the fundamental geopolitical question Ethiopia seeks to answer is: “why would neighbors resist to offer Ethiopia a naval base while accommodating similar needs of other countries?” As the geographic and demographic center, Ethiopian stability has significant repercussions on its neighbors. Ethiopia’s sudden rhetoric on access to the Sea and rapprochement with sub-state actors has distorted its otherwise valid security demand and angered its neighbors. Egypt capitalized on the tension and forged a tripartite agreement with Eritrea and Somalia to contain Ethiopia. Its continued encirclement will only exacerbate regional insecurity. Ethiopia’s untactful rhetoric brought strategic costs: the US and EU firmly backed Somalia’s territorial integrity while maintaining Ethiopia in its counter-terrorism commitments. While Russia quietly monitors shifting alignments, China views Ethiopian ambition with skepticism to protect its extensive Belt and Road Initiative and avoid a precedent for Taiwan. Amid this friction, Türkiye brokered the Ankara Declaration, in which Somalia recognizes Ethiopia’s right to access the sea in exchange for a firm commitment to Somalian unity. Ethiopia’s domestic stability, infrastructure ambitions, and military capabilities remain deeply tied to Gulf backing. Rivalry between Gulf Royals is splitting the region into two informal and fluid “blocs” that function without legal or institutional mechanisms. On one hand, the UAE Bloc comprises Israel and aligns with Ethiopia, Somaliland, RSF (Sudan) and desires to project forward commercial and maritime power in the Red Sea. Conversely, the Saudi bloc includes Egypt, Türkiye, Qatar, Somalia, SAF, and Eritrea and desires to restrict Red Sea governance exclusively to littoral states and deprive other middle powers from gaining a foothold in the region. Ethiopia’s relations with Eritrea deteriorated following the 2022 Pretoria Agreement. In response to Ethiopian sudden shift in rhetoric on sea outlets, Eritrea forges partnership with the Tigray People’s Liberation Front (TPLF) old guards that considered the Pretoria deal as complete surrender. In the face of narrowing political space and shared threat from the federal government, seven Ethiopian political and armed opposition groups, including the TPLF, Amhara Fano, and the Oromo Liberation Army (OLA) forged the Ethiopian People’s Forces Alliance for Survival. The alliance seeks to overthrow the government and establish an inclusive transitional administration. Despite contradictory ideology and various national orientations, the alliance marks a paradigm shift in the Ethiopian fragmented political landscape. Rebels are suppressing their self-contradictions to avoid horizontal conflicts and pave the way for convergence. With fluid institution and organization, the cohesion and success of the alliance would only be tested through time, especially until after it offers a credible alternative and inclusive political architecture, beyond coordinated military operations. Rivalry of Gulf Royals increases asymmetric backing and strategic vulnerabilities. Collaboration with the UAE enabled Ethiopia to render Egyptian-led encirclement ineffective. Yet intensified insurgencies prompted the withdrawal of Ethiopian peacekeeping forces from Somalia. Political survival forces rebel to become conduit for external geopolitical rivalries. Disrupting rebels’ access to foreign lifeline is the only mechanism to force them towards a compromise. If the war on Iran prolongs, declining Gulf attention will force cash-starved Horn economies to shift their alignment. The ongoing crisis is not a conventional civil war fought to control the capital but more of a critical existential dispute over what kind of state Ethiopia must become. While external powers can offer financial, technological, and military aid, they cannot settle deep-seated domestic political fractures. Winning the internal war will enhance Ethiopian regional posture and attractiveness to powers. Yet there is no military solution to domestic political demands. The federal government may possess the operational reach to suppress specific fronts, but it cannot win the broader political war without genuine institutional reform. Even if the federal government successfully suppresses insurgencies, it may still lose the political war. Conversely, while capable of degrading federal military capacity and capturing strategic hubs, the alliance lacks a cohesive political program to govern. Thus, a negotiated political settlement is needed to restore lasting stability. Ethiopia’s regional posture will be significantly enhanced through internal healing and reconciliation. While a political solution must come from within, heavy reliance of Ethiopian political actors on external support only delays dialogue, in the absence of which the International Community (IC) has a moral responsibility to pressure the fragmented domestic actors into a negotiated compromise. In collaboration with the IC, the US and EU should condition debt-restructuring agreements on verified, inclusive, and genuine political dialogue. The US should encourage Egypt and Ethiopia towards a comprehensive “two waters” framework to deescalate regional crises. Under this framework, Ethiopia would appease Egyptian concern over the Nile and GERD in exchange for a reliably negotiated maritime access to the Red Sea. The US should urgently persuade its key regional allies (Saudi and UAE) to cease fueling regional conflicts and restraint from transactional engagement with non-state and sub-state actors. BRICS should complement international diplomatic efforts. Its New Development Bank could foster South-South cooperation by financing a tripartite Nile Water, Energy, and Food Security Nexus to pacify rivalry between its members, Egypt and Ethiopia. The AU, in cooperation with the UN, should challenge the Saudi-led Red Sea Council to revise its exclusionary “littoral states first” governance model and become inclusive to accommodate the needs of vital non-littoral anchor states. The AU, UN, and IGAD should jointly pressurize Ethiopian political actors to agree on comprehensive Security Sector Reform (SSR) and negotiate a Disarmament, Demobilization, and Reintegration (DDR) transitional compact to integrate and stabilize the state and preemptively mitigate the zero-sum “winner’s dilemma” that threatens Ethiopian and reginal security. 1. Introduction Ethiopia continues fighting multiple insurgencies and the current political architecture could not restore political stability and remain a fundamental structural fault line. Lingering armed confrontations consumes Ethiopian meager economy and poses a severe foreign exchange crisis that led to painful currency devaluation. Unresolved debt restructuring negotiations continue aggravating the security crisis. High youth unemployment and civil unrest directly feed into active insurgencies. The humanitarian crisis remains unabated; millions are internally displaced and millions of others are in urgent need of emergency food. While acute malnutrition surpassed the critical threshold, nine million children are out of school. The government’s increased reliance on military response and dwindling political space further exacerbates grievance and erodes state legitimacy. The 2026 election was conducted amidst active conflicts and omitted key jurisdictions. The National Dialogue Commission excluded dominant political and armed groups. Seven political and rebel groups forged the Ethiopian Peoples’ Forces Coalition for Survival or “alliance for survival” that vowed to topple the incumbent government. Recent explosions around the capital and critical military infrastructures raised questions about the governments’ established supremacy in military technology. Ethiopian sudden quest for a Sea outlet added a new height of tensions with neighbors, unsettled Egypt, and entangled internal actors with external adversarial states. The rivalry between Egypt and Ethiopia over the Nile, GERD and the Red Sea creates geopolitical standoffs. The Horn remains in political turmoil and competition for influence along the Red Sea continues proliferating conflicts. Sudanese civil war, intra-Somali political tensions, and Ethiopia’s insurgencies are interconnected with external proxies. Rival middle powers are using transactional deals to pull Horn of Africa nations into proxy alliances, including alliance with non-state actors to contain their adversaries from within. The deal offers internal dissidents access to adversarial states and critical support. Efforts to resolve Ethiopia’s internal political crisis should not exclusively rely on domestic discontents, changing regional dynamics should be accounted for . This paper attempts to map out Ethiopia’s dominant political fractures and their interplay with neighboring states. It evaluates the impact of global power competition, transactional deals by regional rivals, and the efforts of Ethiopian opposition groups to rescue the country, before outlining a modest strategic way-out. 2. Existential Threat: The New Strategy for Political Dominance The term “existential threat” has become a dominant political buzzword in the Red Sea Region. Egypt considers Ethiopian development efforts on the Nile River as a threat to its existence. Ethiopia believes that the lack of access to the Sea threatens Ethiopian national survival. Djibouti views Ethiopian port diversification policy as a threat against its existence. The Sudanese warring factions accuse each other of threatening Sudan’s survival as polity. Somali views Ethiopian Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with Somaliland as unprovoked aggression against its sovereignty and territorial integrity. Eritrea considers Ethiopia’s rhetoric on access to the Sea as a direct threat against its sovereignty. The utilization of existential threat across the region demonstrates the lack of shared regional agenda, shifting alignment, and entanglement with rival foreign states in which one country’s gain is tantamount to another country’s loss. The use and misuse of “existential threat” in the Ethiopian political landscape is even much more nuanced. Political elites’ selective narrative about historical grievance, real or imagined, has transformed political competition into existential threat. Since the politicization of ethnicity as modus operandi, rival political elites portray themselves as defensive while being perceived as offensive by others, making conflict resolutions nearly impossible. Every ethnic group appears to have contradictory memories with varying historical pasts, making it impossible to have a shared collective national memory. Ethnic entrepreneurs perceive losing political control in each group’s territory as equivalent to losing one’s homeland. The fundamental feature that differentiates a genuine existential threat from that of political threat is the evidence based upon which the respective narratives are built. The 1991 victor prescribed ethnocentric arrangement has empowered ethnic groups, promoted vernacular languages, developed local cultures and traditions. However, despite claiming to have redressed historical grievances, the political architecture only shifted and institutionalized inequality and domination. Assigning federating units to a dominant ethnic group intensified victimhood narratives and exacerbated the claim over existential danger when competing for political power. The Ethiopian state currently faces a profound national security dilemma, while the federal government argues that “armed resistance groups threaten national survival,” regional governments counterclaim that “centralization threatens regional autonomy and therefore we must resist.” However, not all existential threat claims are unfounded. For example, persistent atrocities against Amhara and the recent Tigray war made existential politics much more credible. Human Rights Watch documented serious abuse by multiple parties to the conflict which created powerful lessons for every Ethiopian political actor: “If we lose political power, we may eventually lose our physical security” making political compromise harder. If applied, critical analysis could have helped to distinguish between community security, political autonomy, and elite political power competition to identify the scope, extent, and intent of the threat that political elites often present as one and indivisible. Ethnic entrepreneurs view another governance model simply as an existential threat to their own model. Under current arrangement, political programs and policy preferences are not criteria for affinity with political parties and political loyalty remains exclusively dependent on ethnic identity. When each actor is organized politically and militarily to defend itself against the other, the combined effect creates a national security dilemma that forces even groups with contradictory ideology to forge tactical alliances despite lacking a shared national political program. The emergence of “Tsimdo” and alliance for survival follows this pattern. Such alliances remain purely tactical and operational and rarely get strategic height. Therefore, a serious attempt to chart a new political dispensation in Ethiopia must reimagine how the unfolding tactical alliance could be transformed into a veritable national political and security program in which no community feels excluded. 3. Internal Faultlines and vulnerabilities of Contemporary Ethiopia The 2026 National Dialogue Commission deliberation recognized that variation in historical narratives, state building, heritage, institutional design (ethnic vs Unitary), forms of government (religious vs secularism), and loyalties (class vs ethnic) are the fundamental faultlines. The violent insurgencies Ethiopia continues facing today are only manifestations of a much deeper strategic crisis that demand political reimagination and overhaul over state architectures. In the absence of middle ground and compromise, reconfiguration of fundamental state structure remains only desirable. Continued internal disharmony exasperates fragmentation and makes Ethiopia a geopolitical battle ground. Despite the urgency, however, Ethiopian political actors seem to favor zero-sum games. With a growing population of more than 130 million, expanding economy, and asymmetrical military power balance, dependence on a single port increases Ethiopian vulnerability and severely undermines its industrial aspiration. It stresses that denying access to the sea while reaping massive economic benefit by small sized littoral states is inherently unsustainable and unjust. Ethiopia’s sudden and aggressive rhetoric and its controversial dealings with sub-state actors have deeply alienated its neighbors and overshadowed its otherwise valid national interests. Recent Ethiopia’s demand for maritime access is a legitimate and pragmatic path toward regional stability. Lack of favorable response to Ethiopian longstanding predicament will only aggravate regional insecurity. Because Red Sea instability harms Ethiopia more than any of its neighbors combined, temporary commercial leases are simply insufficient. Ethiopia realizes the distinction between commercial and sovereign access and its geopolitical cascading effect. However, it questions why neighboring states failed to offer Ethiopia with a naval base while allowing several other countries. Despite recent calls for peaceful negotiation and dialogue for mutual economic dividend, Ethiopian request remains misconceived and contentious. Immediate neighbors continue viewing Ethiopian positions as an existential threat and establish alliances with the adversaries of Ethiopia and UAE, Ethiopian dominant external enabler. Ethiopia currently stands at a historical crossroad juncture, and its future would be redefined by the following three inextricably linked domestic and regional geopolitical drivers . These dynamics critically determine Ethiopian relations with its neighbors that align with a web of adversarial actors seeking to project influence in the Red Sea region. 1. Accelerating domestic fragmentation isolated and coordinated insurgencies are severely overstretching federal forces, as witnessed by the recent withdrawal announcement of Ethiopian peacekeeping mission from Somalia. Continued failure of the Ethiopian government to harmonize domestic forces will unavoidably exacerbate Ethiopia’s vulnerability and offer regional adversaries with opportunity for aggressive proxy meddling. 2. The Red Sea stalemate Ethiopia’s rhetoric on the Red Sea and framing of permanent, sovereign sea access as a non-negotiable strategic imperative has triggered acute security anxieties among Somalia, Eritrea, and Djibouti and led to shifting regional alignments. Egypt and Saudi are cooperating with other coastal states that view the Red Sea exclusively as littoral states affairs and seek to contain non-coastal states, and 3. The hydrological faultline The completion of GERD and Ethiopia’s continued desire to build three more dams on the Nile River remains an unyielding existential flashpoint for Egypt, and its aggressive move to construct a military and political encirclement of Ethiopia via treaties with Eritrea and Somalia and recently with Sudan. As a credible internal political settlement significantly enhances Ethiopian regional posture, its fragmentation opens doors for meddling. Without a genuine political settlement, Ethiopia will remain in perpetual conflict in which River Nile and the quest for access to the Red Sea serve as catalysts. Only if Ethiopia establishes a legitim",
      "summary": "Ethiopia is grappling with multiple insurgencies, economic distress, and a legitimacy crisis, threatening its national survival. The country, landlocked and reliant on a single port, seeks maritime access to enhance regional integration and security. This quest has heightened tensions with neighbors, particularly Egypt, which has formed alliances with Eritrea and Somalia to counter Ethiopia's ambitions. The geopolitical landscape is further complicated by rivalries among Gulf states, with the UAE supporting Ethiopia while Saudi Arabia aligns with Egypt and its allies. Amidst this turmoil, seven opposition groups have united to form the Ethiopian People's Forces Alliance for Survival, aiming to overthrow the government. The ongoing conflict is not merely a civil war but a struggle over Ethiopia's identity and governance. A political solution is essential for stability, requiring internal dialogue and external pressure from the international community to facilitate negotiations and reforms. Without genuine political engagement, Ethiopia risks further fragmentation and regional insecurity.",
