      "title": "Ethiopia Must Stop Allowing Egypt to Define the Argument - Borkena",
      "link": "https://borkena.com/ethiopia-must-stop-allowing-egypt-to-define-the-argument/",
      "date": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 13:52:15 GMT",
      "image": "https://borkena.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Mohamud-A.-Ahmed-Cagaweyne.webp",
      "content": "Ethiopia Must Stop Allowing Egypt to Define the Argument Home Featured Latest News Business Opinion Video Amharic Entertainment Restaurant Travel Sport Books Blog Business Listings Jobs Support Us Contact Search borkena Ethiopian News and Informed Opinion. Truth Matters! Sign in Welcome! Log into your account your username your password Forgot your password? Get help Create an account Create an account Welcome! Register for an account your email your username A password will be e-mailed to you. Password recovery Recover your password your email A password will be e-mailed to you. borkena Ethiopian News and Opinion Home Featured Latest News Business Opinion Video Amharic Entertainment Restaurant Travel Sport Books Blog Business Listings Jobs Support Us Contact Sign in / Join Monday, October 26, 2026 Facebook Linkedin Telegram TikTok WhatsApp Instagram X Youtube Home Opinion Ethiopia Must Stop Allowing Egypt to Define the Argument Opinion October 26, 2026 Updated: October 26, 2026 Ethiopia Must Stop Allowing Egypt to Define the Argument October 26, 2026 Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp Linkedin Telegram Viber The Nile cannot be governed by inherited privilege, nor can the Horn be stabilized by constraining Ethiopia’s future. Addis Ababa needs a confident African diplomacy that joins national ambition to regional prosperity The author (Archive) By Mohamud A. Ahmed – Cagaweyne The most consequential victory in diplomacy often comes before negotiations begin: the power to decide what the argument is about. Egypt has long understood this. When the Nile dispute is presented principally as a question of Egyptian survival, Ethiopia’s development arrives in the conversation already under suspicion. A dam becomes a threat before it becomes a source of electricity. An upstream population’s aspirations become a problem for downstream officials to assess. Ethiopia is left explaining why its future should be permitted to advance. This is a profoundly unequal starting point. Egypt’s dependence on the Nile is real, and any responsible settlement must protect its people against serious harm. But dependence does not confer ownership. Historical advantage cannot become perpetual jurisdiction over another country’s economic choices. The weakness in Cairo’s approach emerges whenever a legitimate demand for security expands into an expectation of control. Prime Minister Dr. Abiy Ahmed’s recent remarks at the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa offered a constructive foundation for challenging that imbalance. Speaking in Bahir Dar, he placed dependable maritime access within Ethiopia’s long-term security and developmental interests. “For a nation of more than 130 million people, access to the sea is existential,” he said, according to the Ethiopian News Agency’s October 24 report. He also affirmed that Ethiopia would pursue its aspirations through dialogue and mutual benefit. That combination deserves support: clarity about the national interest, coupled with a peaceful method for advancing it. The importance of this argument extends beyond ports. It asks whether Ethiopia’s needs will be treated as enduring realities to accommodate or recurring ambitions to contain. Its population needs reliable electricity, competitive industries, dependable trade routes, and the capacity to withstand external disruption. These requirements will survive changes of government. A regional strategy that assumes Ethiopia can indefinitely absorb the costs of inadequate infrastructure and constrained connectivity mistakes postponed development for permanent acquiescence. National security includes the material foundations of ordinary life. A hospital awaiting equipment, a manufacturer facing uncertain delivery costs, and a farmer seeking access to distant markets all depend upon systems that connect Ethiopia to the world. Abiy’s Tana remarks situated maritime access within that larger concern and linked reliable corridors to African trade and economic opportunity. This provides a persuasive direction for diplomacy: Ethiopia can explain how dependable access would benefit coastal partners, producers and communities along transport routes. Egypt’s leadership should recognize the consistency this demands. A country that asks the world to understand its geographical dependence on the Nile should be prepared to understand Ethiopia’s geographical dependence on transit arrangements. The circumstances differ, but the principle is straightforward. Geography creates vulnerabilities that diplomacy must manage fairly. Cairo weakens its moral authority whenever it seeks broad accommodation for Egyptian necessity while approaching Ethiopian necessity chiefly as a strategic inconvenience. The opening of the ninth Cairo Water Week on October 25 demonstrated Egypt’s sustained capacity to present its priorities internationally. President Abdel-Fattah El-Sisi defended downstream security while acknowledging other basin countries’ development rights. sis.gov.eg . That acknowledgment matters. Its practical value, however, depends upon whether upstream development is allowed to alter an unequal distribution of benefits. A right that can be exercised only when existing beneficiaries approve its consequences is precariously close to a privilege granted at their discretion. Cairo’s diplomatic strength lies partly in continuity. Political leaders establish the message; technical institutions elaborate it; diplomats repeat it; conferences and media carry it to wider audiences. Ethiopia should study that discipline. It should also challenge the selective reasoning that can accompany it. The repeated invocation of Egyptian vulnerability does not settle how competing needs should be reconciled. Repetition may establish familiarity, but familiarity is no substitute for an equitable argument. The essential arithmetic is modest: Egypt’s security cannot require Ethiopia’s development to approach zero. Any arrangement built upon that assumption would contain the seeds of its own failure. The basin’s political equation has several populations, several economies and several legitimate claims. A settlement that recognizes only one country’s indispensable needs leaves too many variables outside the calculation. Those excluded interests eventually return as mistrust, resistance and renewed dispute. Ethiopia should therefore begin with the human purpose of the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam. Electricity means a functioning clinic, a productive workshop, a student able to study after sunset and an enterprise capable of expanding. These aspirations deserve the same moral seriousness as downstream concerns. Poverty should never become the invisible baseline against which another country’s security is measured. An Ethiopian household’s exclusion from reliable power is also a deprivation, even when it produces fewer international headlines. At Tana, Dr. Abiy appropriately connected the dam’s achievement to the obligation to deliver reliable electricity, industrial growth, and improved livelihoods. www.ena.et That is the standard Ethiopian leadership should accept. National achievement acquires lasting authority through public benefit. A dam’s significance rests in the opportunities it creates, and Ethiopia’s international case becomes stronger when its domestic performance makes those opportunities visible. Yet a sound Ethiopian argument must examine operational risks rather than assume that a legitimate purpose resolves them. Reservoir management, prolonged drought and downstream planning require evidence and workable cooperation. Ethiopia should support useful information exchange, credible technical assessment and practical procedures for managing disagreement. Such measures demonstrate responsible authority. They also make it harder to turn uncertainty into an unlimited argument against Ethiopian development. Egypt bears a corresponding responsibility: it must distinguish demonstrable risks from demands that preserve its preferred position indefinitely. A request for dependable drought safeguards deserves serious negotiation. An expectation that upstream development must leave every downstream advantage untouched deserves challenge. Existing use matters, but treating it as an immovable ceiling on everyone else’s future would freeze inequality into the rules of cooperation. The United Nations Watercourses Convention provides an important reference through its principles of equitable and reasonable utilization, cooperation and prevention of significant harm. Applying those principles requires attention to relevant law, agreements and circumstances; citing them does not decide every operational dispute. Their broader significance is clear, however: shared water governance requires consideration of multiple interests. It cannot responsibly be reduced to unlimited upstream discretion or permanent downstream predominance. Ethiopia’s strongest response would combine legal precision with scientific openness. Rainfall totals, for example, reveal little without examining seasonality, storage, accessibility and infrastructure. Equally, claims about a dam’s regional benefits require scrutiny of operating conditions. The country should publish credible analysis, acknowledge uncertainty and invite serious examination. Evidence gives diplomacy a foundation that rhetorical escalation cannot supply. Ethiopia does not need to exaggerate its rights to establish that they matter. Maritime access requires the same discipline. Article 125 of the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea recognizes access and transit rights for landlocked states while requiring agreement on their exercise and protecting transit states’ legitimate interests. It provides a framework for cooperation, not an automatic entitlement to another country’s territory or a particular port. www.un.org Ethiopia can pursue commercial agreements, infrastructure partnerships, leases and dependable transit arrangements through consent. The Prime Minister’s emphasis on dialogue offers the appropriate political foundation. This makes categorical resistance to Ethiopia’s lawful maritime aspirations especially shortsighted. A dependable corridor can generate port revenue, infrastructure investment, employment, and larger markets. The commercial logic is additive: greater Ethiopian activity can enlarge opportunities for coastal economies. Governments should negotiate the distribution of those gains, rather than assume that Ethiopia’s improved access necessarily diminishes their sovereignty. Properly designed agreements can protect both sovereignty and commercial reliability. Recent coordination among Egypt, Eritrea, Somalia and Sudan warrants careful Ethiopian assessment. Such alignment does not, by itself, prove a covert strategy of destabilization. Nevertheless, any approach that treats constraining Ethiopia as a durable source of regional advantage would be a grave miscalculation. An economically frustrated and politically insecure Horn would offer Egypt little dependable reassurance. Pressure that deepens mistrust can make the cooperation Cairo seeks harder to obtain. Egypt should resist the temptation to treat every Ethiopian gain as an Egyptian loss. That assumption turns diplomacy into a room with narrowing walls. It encourages tactical obstruction while neglecting the larger interest in a capable upstream partner able to negotiate, share information, and implement commitments. Ethiopia’s instability would make those tasks more difficult. Predictability depends upon institutions, and institutions require a viable economic and political foundation. Ethiopia must draw the corresponding lesson at home. National cohesion, accountable government, and administrative competence strengthen external influence. Its citizens deserve institutions that convert national projects into public opportunity and diplomatic commitments into reliable performance. Confidence abroad becomes more credible when supported by competence at home. The defense of Ethiopian interests therefore requires investment in domestic legitimacy alongside forceful international advocacy. It also requires intellectual infrastructure. Ethiopia needs hydrologists who can test scenarios, lawyers who can explain competing obligations, economists who can evaluate corridor arrangements, and scholars who can assess regional developments independently. A credible research institution devoted to Nile cooperation and the Horn’s economic security could contribute substantially. Its value would depend upon transparent methods and freedom to publish inconvenient findings. Scholarship loses authority when its conclusions are assigned in advance. Universities, professional associations, civil society organizations and diaspora specialists should help sustain that effort. Ethiopia should engage the strongest Egyptian arguments, identify weaknesses in its own policies and produce proposals that withstand scrutiny. Knowledge must accumulate across changes of personnel. A country should not have to reconstruct its case whenever a neighboring minister delivers a speech. Tana can support this continuity by connecting African expertise with the practical responsibilities of government. The continental argument must be equally confident. Pan-Africanism cannot mean preserving an inherited hierarchy in which some societies retain established benefits while others are asked to postpone transformation. Nor can it disregard Egyptian vulnerability. Egypt belongs within the African community whose future Ethiopia invokes. A credible African settlement would protect downstream lives while recognizing upstream development as a legitimate and necessary part of continental progress. Dr. Abiy’s Tana remarks deserve support because they connect an enduring Ethiopian priority to dialogue and mutual benefit. Their promise now requires patient negotiation, technically credible proposals, and agreements that partners can trust. Ethiopia should build upon that foundation to present a coherent regional vision: electricity that supports production, corridors that sustain commerce, and institutions that make cooperation dependable. Cairo can help shape that future, but it must reconsider an approach that too readily confuses Ethiopian advancement with Egyptian danger. The Nile cannot become a permanent instrument for rationing another nation’s aspirations. Ethiopia, for its part, should defend its interests with the confidence to negotiate and the discipline to make its proposals credible. Egypt’s water security deserves protection. Ethiopia’s development deserves room to advance. A durable settlement must be built around both realities. About the Author Mohamud A. Ahmed – Cagaweyne is a Columnist, Political and Security Analyst, Researcher and Greenlight Advisors Group. He lives in the Somali Region of Ethiopia and could be reached at +251 900 644 648 or +254 759 717 898( WhatsApp) Editor’s Note: Views in the article do not necessarily reflect the views of borkena.com __ Join our Telegram Channel: t.me/borkena Like borkena on Facebook To submit a press release, send the submission to info@borkena.com Add your business to Ethiopian Business Listing / Ethiopian Business Directory Join the conversation. Follow us on X (formerly Twitter) @zborkena to get the latest Ethiopian news updates regularly. Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp Linkedin Telegram Viber Tags Egypt Ethiopia Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp Linkedin Telegram Viber Facebook Instagram WhatsApp Telegram X Youtube advertisment LEAVE A REPLY Cancel reply Comment: Please enter your comment! Name:* Please enter your name here Email:* You have entered an incorrect email address! Please enter your email address here Website: Save my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment. borkena Ethiopian News and Informed Opinion. Truth Matters! Canada Africa Guest Post Sponsored Sitemap Europe Latin America Middle East & Asia Photography Business Listing Free Classified Ads Editorial Policy Disclaimer Facebook Instagram Linkedin Telegram TikTok X Website WhatsApp Youtube",
      "summary": "Ethiopia is urged to redefine its diplomatic stance regarding the Nile dispute, which has been dominated by Egypt's narrative of survival. The article emphasizes that Ethiopia's development should not be viewed as a threat but rather as a necessity for its population of over 130 million. Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed's recent remarks at the Tana High-Level Forum highlight the importance of maritime access for Ethiopia's security and development, framing it as a mutual benefit for regional partners. The author argues that Egypt's historical advantages should not translate into perpetual control over Ethiopia's economic choices. A balanced approach is essential, recognizing both countries' needs and fostering cooperation rather than conflict. The article calls for Ethiopia to present its case with clarity and evidence, advocating for equitable water governance that respects the interests of all basin countries. Ultimately, a collaborative strategy is necessary to ensure regional stability and shared prosperity. (151 words)",
