      "content": "Ethiopian Passport Ranks Among World's Lowest, Limiting Travel to 17 Visa-Free Destinations - allAfrica.com AllAfrica English (current) En Français Toggle navigation My Account Toggle navigation AllAfrica My Account English (current) En Français Ethiopian Passport Ranks Among World's Lowest, Limiting Travel to 17 Visa-Free Destinations Ethiopian Passport Services Ethiopian passport. 26 October 2026 Capital FM (Nairobi) By Bruhan Makong Nairobi — Ethiopian passport holders can travel to only 17 countries without a visa, according to figures attributed to the 2026 Global Passport Power Index, highlighting barriers facing Ethiopian citizens seeking international business, education and cultural opportunities. The index places Ethiopia at position 90 globally, alongside countries including Somalia, Yemen, Syria and Afghanistan, according to the report cited by Passport Rank. Ethiopian citizens can access a further 33 destinations through visas on arrival, while only five African Union member states -- Kenya, Benin, The Gambia, Rwanda and Togo -- are listed as allowing them visa-free entry. Follow us on WhatsApp | LinkedIn for the latest headlines The ranking reflects the restrictions Ethiopian travelers face when seeking to cross borders, with advance visa applications, extensive documentation and processing delays potentially complicating international travel. These barriers can affect businesses seeking to attend trade exhibitions, negotiate deals or build partnerships abroad, as well as students and researchers pursuing academic exchanges and other international opportunities. The report attributes the passport's low standing to a combination of economic conditions, security concerns and diplomatic relations. It says foreign governments commonly consider income levels, security risks, and the likelihood of visa overstays when determining entry requirements. Regional instability and security concerns in the Horn of Africa are also cited as factors contributing to strict visa screening for Ethiopian travelers. The report further points to limited reciprocal visa-waiver arrangements as a constraint on mobility. The restrictions can create challenges for Ethiopian companies seeking to expand internationally, particularly where foreign businesspeople can enter Ethiopia through e-visas or visas on arrival while Ethiopian nationals must undergo more demanding visa procedures to visit potential markets. The report says the consequences extend beyond commerce to academic cooperation, cultural exchanges, and international engagement. Read the original article on Capital FM . Tagged: Ethiopia East Africa Travel Copyright © 2026 Capital FM . All rights reserved. Distributed by AllAfrica Global Media ( allAfrica.com ). To contact the copyright holder directly for corrections — or for permission to republish or make other authorized use of this material, click here. AllAfrica publishes around 600 reports a day from more than 90 news organizations and over 500 other institutions and individuals , representing a diversity of positions on every topic. We publish news and views ranging from vigorous opponents of governments to government publications and spokespersons. Publishers named above each report are responsible for their own content, which AllAfrica does not have the legal right to edit or correct. Articles and commentaries that identify allAfrica.com as the publisher are produced or commissioned by AllAfrica . To address comments or complaints, please Contact us . AllAfrica is a voice of, by and about Africa - aggregating, producing and distributing 600 news and information items daily from over 90 African news organizations and our own reporters to an African and global public. We operate from Cape Town, Dakar, Abuja, Johannesburg, Nairobi and Washington DC. Support our work Sign up for our newsletter For Advertisers Subscribe © 2026 AllAfrica Privacy Policy Contact Us About Us AllAfrica - All the Time Close Sign up for free AllAfrica Newsletters Get the latest in African news delivered straight to your inbox Submit By submitting above, you agree to our privacy policy . Success! Almost finished... We need to confirm your email address. To complete the process, please follow the instructions in the email we just sent you. Error! Error! There was a problem processing your submission. Please try again later.",
      "summary": "Ethiopian passport holders face significant travel limitations, ranking 90th globally on the 2026 Global Passport Power Index, alongside countries like Somalia and Yemen. They can only visit 17 countries without a visa and access an additional 33 destinations through visas on arrival. Among African Union member states, only five—Kenya, Benin, The Gambia, Rwanda, and Togo—offer visa-free entry to Ethiopians. The report highlights that stringent visa requirements, extensive documentation, and processing delays hinder international travel for Ethiopian citizens, impacting business, education, and cultural exchanges. Factors contributing to this low ranking include economic conditions, security concerns, and diplomatic relations, with foreign governments often considering income levels and security risks when setting entry requirements. The lack of reciprocal visa-waiver arrangements further complicates mobility, creating challenges for Ethiopian businesses and individuals seeking opportunities abroad. These restrictions not only affect commerce but also academic cooperation and international engagement.",
