      "content": "World News in Brief: Ethiopians flee to Kenya amidst renewed conflict, new head of UN’s food programme, UN mission in Somalia concludes | UN News Skip to main content / navigation Welcome to the United Nations Language العربية 中文 English Français Русский Español Português Kiswahili Other Hindi हिंदी Urdu اردو Global UN News Global perspective Human stories English Search Search Advanced Search Language العربية 中文 English Français Русский Español Português Kiswahili Other Hindi हिंदी Urdu اردو Global UN News Global perspective Human stories Menu Home Global Africa Americas Asia Pacific Middle East Europe UN Art and Gifts History Corner Topics Peace and Security Economic Development Humanitarian Aid Climate and Environment Human Rights UN Affairs Women Law and Crime Prevention Health Culture and Education SDGs Migrants and Refugees In depth In Focus Interviews Features Photo Stories UN News Today The Lid is On UN Gender Focus UN and Africa UN Podcasts Secretary-General Spokesperson All Statements Selected Speeches Press Encounters Official Travels Media UN Video UN Photo Meeting Coverage Media Accreditation UN Web TV Language العربية 中文 English Français Русский Español Português Kiswahili Other Hindi हिंदी Urdu اردو Global Search Search Advanced Search Audio and Subscription Audio Hub Subscribe World News in Brief: Ethiopians flee to Kenya amidst renewed conflict, new head of UN’s food programme, UN mission in Somalia concludes © Kenya Red Cross People who fled fighting in Moyale, Ethiopia, gather at a reception area in Kenya. Facebook Twitter Print Email World News in Brief: Ethiopians flee to Kenya amidst renewed conflict, new head of UN’s food programme, UN mission in Somalia concludes 26 October 2026 Peace and Security Around 36,600 people have fled into northern Kenya following an uptick in fighting on the Ethiopian side of the border. That’s according to the UN migration agency, IOM . Forces from the Somali and Oromia regions in Southern Ethiopia have been fighting for over two decades over boundary demarcations and resources, with recent escalations since last week sparking a new wave of displacement. Amongst those who fled, nearly 11,000 have sought sanctuary within the large compound of the Oriental Mission Church (OMC), a prominent Korean-American Christian organisation based in Los Angeles, which does missionary and humanitarian work in Kenya, said IOM. Community support The remaining 25,600 people are being hosted by communities along the border. IOM is working with the Kenyan government and the UN refugee agency, UNHCR , to collect and manage displacement data, as well as assess humanitarian needs. So far it has identified needs for water and sanitation, shelter and essential household items, food assistance, health support and protection. It also stresses the need to pay special attention to the needs of host communities, who are accommodating most of the displaced, despite already limited resources. Lindberg takes helm at WFP with call to end famine Tweet URL Luke J. Lindberg took office as Executive Director of the UN World Food Programme ( WFP ) on Monday, calling on donors, partners and supporters worldwide to help sustain the agency’s life-saving work. “Conflict is driving people into hunger, and extreme weather and maritime choke points exacerbate this reality,” Mr. Lindberg said. “We cannot do it alone. Governments, institutions, businesses, and individuals need to stand with us, fund the work, and help get food to people before it’s too late. No more famines.” Millions in need As many as 266 million people faced acute hunger worldwide last year, with conflict driving the most severe crises. WFP teams currently operate in 83 countries. Mr. Lindberg succeeds Cindy McCain, who led WFP from April 2023 to May 2026, and Carl Skau, who has served as Acting Executive Director since June. He previously served as Under Secretary for Trade and Foreign Agricultural Affairs at the US Department of Agriculture. Somalia takes the lead as UN mission bows out after three decades The UN’s political mission in Somalia will close on Saturday , ending more than three decades of UN missions in the country supporting peace, state-building and reconciliation. Tweet URL The UN Transitional Assistance Mission in Somalia (UNTMIS) completes its two-year mandate on 31 October. The Security Council has decided not to renew it, at the request of the Somali Government, which wants to take greater ownership of its own peacebuilding and state-building. The mission’s work will pass to Somali institutions and to the UN agencies, funds and programmes already operating in the country. Tribute to those who served Secretary-General António Guterres praised the progress made over the decades and thanked those who made it possible, in particular his successive Special Representatives and the mission’s staff. He also paid tribute to UN personnel who lost their lives while serving in Somalia. Mr. Guterres acknowledged that Somalia still faces serious political, security, humanitarian and development challenges, and called on the international community to keep up its support so that the gains made are not lost. That means pushing forward with inclusive political dialogue and reconciliation, building stronger institutions, protecting human rights and confronting Al Shabaab, the Somalia-based terrorist group affiliated with al-Qaeda. ♦ Receive daily updates directly in your inbox - Subscribe here to a topic. ♦ Download the UN News app for your iOS or Android devices. Ethiopia Kenya Related Stories Latest deadly shipwreck highlights need for safer migration World News in Brief: Airliner safety warning, drought in Somalia, solar boosts farming in Ethiopia World News in Brief: UN scales up Ebola response, refugees ‘exiled’ in Africa, Political tensions escalate in Somalia Resources Button collapse Navigate the News Information for Broadcasters UN System Links Media Alert UN Journal Meetings Coverage Audiovisual Library Secretary-General Button collapse All Statements Official Travels Press Encounters SG Twitter Spokesperson's Office Button collapse Latest Statements Briefing Highlights Briefing Transcripts Notes to Correspondents Find Us Button collapse UN News App Facebook Twitter YouTube Footer menu A-Z Site Index Copyright FAQ Fraud Alert Privacy Notice Terms of Use Social Media Links Footer buttons Donate Facebook Twitter Print Email",
      "summary": "Amidst escalating conflict in Ethiopia, approximately 36,600 individuals have fled to northern Kenya, according to the International Organization for Migration (IOM). The violence, primarily between forces from the Somali and Oromia regions over boundary disputes and resources, has intensified recently, prompting a new wave of displacement. Nearly 11,000 refugees have found shelter at the Oriental Mission Church, while the remaining 25,600 are being hosted by local communities. IOM is collaborating with the Kenyan government and UNHCR to assess humanitarian needs, focusing on water, sanitation, shelter, and food assistance. In other news, Luke J. Lindberg has assumed the role of Executive Director of the UN World Food Programme, emphasizing the urgent need for global support to combat hunger exacerbated by conflict and climate issues. Additionally, the UN's political mission in Somalia will conclude after over three decades, transferring responsibilities to Somali institutions, as the government seeks greater control over its peacebuilding efforts.",
