      "title": "UN mission prepares to exit Somalia after over three-decade support - TRT Afrika",
      "link": "https://www.trtafrika.com/english/article/9074c9fe524f",
      "date": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:32:46 GMT",
      "image": "https://d341l694dl30ad.cloudfront.net/images/aa/2026/07/29/6a69d7b2057799567e86c0cd/original/88fc5499-8fe3-4bf9-a83b-0ae51b52e7e0-1785321394531.jpg?width=1080&amp;format=jpg&amp;quality=80",
      "content": "UN mission prepares to exit Somalia after over three-decade support - TRT Afrika | English | English POLITICS AFRICA TÜRKİYE OPINION FEATURES SPORTS AFRICA 2 min read UN mission prepares to exit Somalia after over three-decade support The mission would cease operations in accordance with UN Security Council resolutions and a request from Somalia’s government, said a spokesman. Share FILE NATION: United Nations Secretary-General Antonio Guterres. The UN Transitional Assistance Mission in Somalia (UNTMIS) will conclude its mandate on October 31, ending a two-year transition and more than three decades of UN missions supporting peacebuilding, governance and state-building in the country, a UN official said Monday. In a statement, UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres’ spokesman, Stephane Dujarric, said the mission would cease operations in accordance with Security Council resolutions and a request from Somalia’s federal government. “This milestone brings to a close more than three decades of United Nations missions in support of peacebuilding, state-building, governance and reconciliation in Somalia,” Dujarric said. The closure follows a jointly agreed transition roadmap under which UNTMIS’s mandated responsibilities have been transferred to Somali institutions and UN agencies, funds and programmes. RECOMMENDED South Africa ambassador calls for 'direct dialogue' with US amid tensions Algeria's president authorises troop deployment to protect Niger's energy sites Ebola in Nigeria: Patient evacuated to Switzerland, condition stable How virtual reality is helping Uganda's youth step into the workplace RECOMMENDED South Africa ambassador calls for 'direct dialogue' with US amid tensions Algeria's president authorises troop deployment to protect Niger's energy sites Ebola in Nigeria: Patient evacuated to Switzerland, condition stable How virtual reality is helping Uganda's youth step into the workplace Agencies and programmes Guterres reaffirmed that the UN would continue supporting Somalia through its resident agencies and programmes, working with Somali authorities, the African Union and other international partners to promote peace, stability, human rights and sustainable development, Dujarric said. “Recognising the significant political, security, humanitarian and development challenges that remain, the Secretary-General calls for sustained international support to consolidate progress, advance inclusive political dialogue and reconciliation, strengthen institutions, and promote and protect human rights,” he added. Dujarric stressed that Guterres reaffirmed the UN’s commitment to the people of Somalia and to supporting Somali-led efforts to build a peaceful, stable and prosperous future. SOURCE : َAA RECOMMENDED South Africa ambassador calls for 'direct dialogue' with US amid tensions Algeria's president authorises troop deployment to protect Niger's energy sites Ebola in Nigeria: Patient evacuated to Switzerland, condition stable How virtual reality is helping Uganda's youth step into the workplace RECOMMENDED South Africa ambassador calls for 'direct dialogue' with US amid tensions Algeria's president authorises troop deployment to protect Niger's energy sites Ebola in Nigeria: Patient evacuated to Switzerland, condition stable How virtual reality is helping Uganda's youth step into the workplace Explore Tanzanian opposition leader facing treason charges, Tundu Lissu, threatens to go on hunger strike Gunmen kill dozens of people in Nigeria's northeastern state of Taraba Uganda to impose 21-day quarantine on returning citizens with DRC travel history Senegal's PASTEF party rejects revised budget proposed by Faye's government Nigeria's Tinubu launches re-election bid, claims rivals 'created hunger, poverty' South Sudan: At least 10 people killed in attacks linked to cattle rustling At least 22 people killed in Kenya after bus and truck collide on major highway Resumption of war would not change Iran's stance: Araghchi Thousands of Israelis flood Tel Aviv streets to demand end of Netanyahu era ahead of elections Gabon restores social media after eight-month ban Police fire tear gas as new French demonstrations kick off US intel memo flags 'advanced' Iranian plots to target American diplomats abroad On UN Day, Türkiye's Erdogan calls for 'just, inclusive' Security Council Senegal's majority party PASTEF rejects budget in committee stage Netanyahu says end of US military support to Israel is 'only a matter of time' on Copyright © 2026 TRT Afrika English. TV Schedule Contact Us Careers Terms Of Use Privacy Policy Cookie Policy Follow TRT Afrika English on Copyright © 2026 TRT Afrika English.",
      "summary": "The United Nations Transitional Assistance Mission in Somalia (UNTMIS) is set to conclude its operations on October 31, marking the end of over three decades of UN support for peacebuilding and governance in Somalia. This decision follows a request from the Somali government and aligns with UN Security Council resolutions. UN spokesperson Stephane Dujarric emphasized that this transition reflects a roadmap agreed upon to transfer responsibilities to Somali institutions and various UN agencies. Despite the mission's closure, UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres assured continued support for Somalia through resident agencies, focusing on promoting peace, stability, and human rights. He called for sustained international assistance to address ongoing political, security, and humanitarian challenges, while reinforcing the UN's commitment to aiding Somali-led efforts for a peaceful and prosperous future. The mission's exit signifies a significant milestone in Somalia's journey towards self-governance and stability.",
