      "image": "https://www.hiiraan.com/uploads/2026/10/1792990497ecc02c44-e8b4-4703-be98-777b6e604484.jpg",
      "content": "Somalia releases Ebola contact after two negative tests, 21-day quarantine - Hiiraan Online Jobs | Twitter | Somali Links | RSS Dark Mode Hiiraan Online HOME OPINION POLITICS RSS CONTACT US FACEBOOK × OPINION WARARKA MAANTA SPORTS SECURITY POLITICS Privacy Policy Contact Us Jobs advertisements HOME NEWS Somalia releases Ebola contact after two negative tests, 21-day quarantine Share Facebook Messenger Twitter WhatsApp LinkedIn Telegram Copy Link Email Font Size A- A A+ Monday October 26, 2026 Mogadishu (HOL) — Somali Health Ministry released a man from Ebola quarantine after two tests returned negative and he completed a 21-day monitoring period without developing symptoms, officials announced Sunday. The man had been placed in isolation after health authorities determined that he had direct contact with a person infected with Bundibugyo Ebola virus disease who later died in Kenya. advertisements “Following up on the statement issued on October 12, 2026, the Ministry shares with the Somali public the latest information regarding the Somali citizen who was placed under quarantine,” the Health Ministry said. The ministry said the man was transferred on Oct. 12 to an Ebola quarantine and treatment center at Forlanini Hospital in Mogadishu, where he was monitored under infection-control protocols. He underwent two Ebola tests, one conducted in Somalia and another sent abroad for confirmation. Both returned negative, the ministry said. The man also remained free of Ebola symptoms throughout the quarantine period and completed 21 days of monitoring, the maximum incubation period generally used for Ebola contact follow-up. “Taking into account the results of both tests, the completion of the monitoring period, the citizen’s health status, and an assessment of public health risks ... the Ministry of Health has decided to release the citizen from quarantine,” the statement said. Somalia’s Health Ministry also stressed that no Ebola cases have been confirmed in the country. Health Minister Dr. Ali Haji Adan said authorities would continue surveillance and monitoring as the regional outbreak develops. The exposure was linked to Kenya’s first confirmed case of Bundibugyo Ebola virus disease, an imported case involving a man who had traveled from the Democratic Republic of Congo through Uganda before arriving in Nairobi on Oct. 3. He tested positive on Oct. 5 and died later that day, according to the World Health Organization. Kenyan authorities initially identified dozens of contacts connected to the case and placed high-risk contacts under quarantine. By Oct. 20, Kenya had reported nine Bundibugyo Ebola cases, including deaths, all linked to the original imported case, according to WHO. Somali health authorities have not reported any locally acquired or imported confirmed Ebola infection. More News Somali president invites opposition groups to national election talks in Mogadishu HOL Heavy fighting erupts near Baidoa between rival South West State forces HOL Three Somali electricity companies raise tariffs by 5 cents amid rising fuel costs HOL Former NISA chief meets Deni, Madobe and Irro in UAE over alliance against Mohamud HOL 4 rescued on third day after Kenya building collapse as families lose hope AP Review: Mbappe decides Clasico, Real Sociedad goes fifth in Liga Xinhua Kenya bus crash kills at least 24 as government vows road safety crackdown Al Jazeera About HOL Privacy Policy Contact Us Jobs Categories Opinion Somali Links Follow Us All Rights Reserved Copyright © 1999-2026, www.hiiraan.com Page created on: 2026-10-26 22:30:18 UTC",
      "summary": "Somalia's Health Ministry announced the release of a man from Ebola quarantine after he tested negative twice and completed a 21-day monitoring period without showing any symptoms. The individual had been isolated following direct contact with a person infected with the Bundibugyo Ebola virus, who later died in Kenya. He was transferred to an Ebola treatment center in Mogadishu on October 12, where he underwent two tests—one locally and another sent abroad for confirmation—both of which returned negative. The ministry confirmed that no Ebola cases have been reported in Somalia. Health Minister Dr. Ali Haji Adan emphasized the importance of ongoing surveillance as the regional outbreak evolves, particularly in light of Kenya's confirmed cases linked to the original imported infection. As of October 20, Kenya had reported nine cases, including fatalities, related to this outbreak. Somali health authorities continue to monitor the situation closely.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/somalia-releases-ebola-contact-after-two-negative-tests-21-d-c22f8ffcc7.html"
    },
