      "content": "Arab News | Regional forces clash with federal troops in southern Somalia Saudi Arabia News The Place The Space Who's Who KSA Today Green & Blue Middle East World Business Insight Energy Finance Giga-Projects Mining Startups Tourism & Transport Eye On AI Media Lifestyle Art & Culture Fashion Offbeat Travel Entertainment Food & Health The Six Books & Gaming Opinion Sport Saudi Football Saudi Pro League Football Motorsport Tennis Cricket Golf Research & Studies Research Papers YouGov Deep Dive Preachers of Hate Podcast Arab News Arab News PK Arab News JP Arab News FR Tuesday . October 27, 2026 Saudi Arabia News The Place The Space Who's Who KSA Today Green & Blue Middle East World Business Insight Energy Finance Giga-Projects Mining Startups Tourism & Transport Eye On AI Media Lifestyle Art & Culture Fashion Offbeat Travel Entertainment Food & Health The Six Books & Gaming Opinion Sport Saudi Football Saudi Pro League Football Motorsport Tennis Cricket Golf Research & Studies Research Papers YouGov Deep Dive Preachers of Hate Podcast LATEST NEWS / Middle East Israel’s election could reshape its politics, without changing its course on Palestine You are here Home regional forces clash with federal troops in southern somalia Regional forces clash with federal troops in southern Somalia In this photo taken with a smartphone, security personnel patrol a street in Baidoa, Somalia, Thursday, Sept. 3, 2026. (AP Photo) Short Url https://arab.news/p7fd9 Updated 26 October 2026 19:25 afp October 26, 2026 19:22 Follow President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud sent in federal troops to oust the head of South West State Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen Laftagareen has launched repeated attempts to retake the region and its main city of Baidoa Updated 26 October 2026 19:25 afp October 26, 2026 19:22 Follow MOGADISHU: Forces loyal to an ousted regional leader in southwestern Somalia again clashed with federal soldiers near the outskirts of Baidoa city, officials and witnesses said Monday. Several states in the deeply fragmented country are opposed to control by President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, and in March his government sent in troops to oust the head of South West State, Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen. He has launched repeated attempts to retake the region and its main city of Baidoa, most recently in September when thousands of his forces attacked, leaving at least three people dead. The fighting erupted as Somali security forces established new defensive positions in Burjeedka, a small settlement near the city. “A large-scale operation targeting terrorist groups began this morning,” South West State’s information minister Nur Siidow Baaba told reporters. He said federal forces were advancing in several directions “to restore state authority in areas recently lost.” “We inflicted heavy casualties, while two of our soldiers were killed,” said Somali army commander Abdullahi Hassan. But a pro-Laftagareen commander, Hassan Abdulkadir Mohamed, claimed his forces were in control. “A unit of our Darwiish forces clashed with enemy troops from Mogadishu, forcing them into a humiliating retreat to Baidoa,” he said, without providing more details. Witnesses in Baidoa reported hearing heavy fighting along the road to Mogadishu early on Monday. “We heard loud explosions and saw a convoy of reinforcements leaving the city toward the fighting. Gunfire continued for more than an hour,” said resident Nur Yare. The full casualty toll remains unknown, although both sides acknowledged losses in the clash. Last week, at least three members of a pro-Laftagareen militia were killed by airstrikes in the Wajid district, Bakool region. Follow Topics: Somalia Baidoa R elated Regional forces capture key towns in southern Somalia US-Somali airstrikes kill Al-Shabab militants, hit weapons ship, government says L atest updates Trump slams ‘rigged’ Brazil election after ally loses to leftist Lula Ex-FBI chief and Trump critic James Comey hit with fresh charges Syrian Airlines set to resume Berlin-Damascus flights in November Trump approval rises from record low to 32 percent ahead of midterm election, Reuters Ipsos poll finds Trump pledges $1 mn for info leading to vote fraud conviction Israeli settler security chief granted house arrest after being charged with shooting Palestinian woman L atest Updates Trump slams ‘rigged’ Brazil election after ally loses to leftist Lula Ex-FBI chief and Trump critic James Comey hit with fresh charges Trump approval rises from record low to 32 percent ahead of midterm election, Reuters Ipsos poll finds Trump pledges $1 mn for info leading to vote fraud conviction Mystery money floods US midterm election campaigns Saudi Arabia Middle East World Business Sport Lifestyle Opinion Media Jobs Contact Us Advertise Privacy & Terms of Service Corporate and Sponsored Content © 2026 Arab News EMAIL ALERTS Stay on top of the issues that matter to you the most. Subscribe Now",
      "summary": "In southern Somalia, regional forces loyal to ousted leader Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen clashed with federal troops near Baidoa city. This conflict follows President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud's deployment of federal troops in March to remove Laftagareen from power. Despite previous attempts to regain control, including a significant assault in September that resulted in casualties, Laftagareen's forces continue to challenge the federal government. On Monday, fighting intensified as Somali security forces established new defensive positions in Burjeedka, with both sides claiming to have inflicted losses on the other. Witnesses reported heavy gunfire and explosions, indicating a fierce confrontation. The situation remains fluid, with the full extent of casualties yet to be determined. The ongoing clashes highlight the deep fragmentation and instability within Somalia, as various regions resist central government authority.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/regional-forces-clash-with-federal-troops-in-southern-somali-c86059a6ae.html"
    },
